# NeurIPS 2026 submission requirements for this delivery

This is the venue brief for the objective in `venue-repo`#1: deliver a complete paper that
could be submitted to **NeurIPS 2026** (main track), written to the venue's submission
requirements, consisting of the LaTeX source in the NeurIPS template, the compiled PDF, and
the code and experimental results the paper's claims rest on.

**Venue:** The Fortieth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems
(NeurIPS 2026), **Main Track**, double-blind reviewing.

**Sources** (all accessed 2026-10-11 through the `neurips-and-ctan` resource; verbatim
quotations below are from these documents):

1. Call for Papers: https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/CallForPapers
2. Main Track Handbook V2026.3: https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/MainTrackHandbook
3. Official formatting instructions and style files:
   https://media.neurips.cc/Conferences/NeurIPS2026/Formatting_Instructions_For_NeurIPS_2026.zip
   (contains `neurips_2026.tex`, `neurips_2026.sty`, `checklist.tex`; the same three files,
   byte-identical, are in `template/` of this repository)

Requirements marked **[binding]** are enforced on the delivered submission through
`venue-artifacts.json` (declared required paths) and this brief.

## 1. What the delivered submission consists of

In the venue's own terms (Main Track Handbook, "Paper Formatting Instructions"):

> All submissions must be in PDF format, and in a single PDF file include, in this suggested order
> - The submitted paper content
> - Paper references
> - Appendices containing any other supporting textual information e.g. proofs, experimental details (code and data will be submitted in a separate file).
> - The NeurIPS paper checklist

Supplementary material ("Code and Data Submission"): "may also be submitted separately in a
single ZIP file of up to 100MB ... and, as with the main submission, be anonymized."

**[binding] Delivered paths.** This delivery maps the objective's named artifacts to the
following paths, declared in `venue-artifacts.json` and required to be nonempty in the
approved delivery:

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `paper.tex` | Main LaTeX source of the paper, built on the NeurIPS 2026 template |
| `neurips_2026.sty` | The official NeurIPS 2026 style file, unmodified |
| `checklist.tex` | The completed NeurIPS paper checklist, `\input` from `paper.tex` |
| `paper.pdf` | The compiled PDF of the paper (single PDF: content, references, appendices, checklist) |
| `code/` | The code the paper's claims rest on (the venue's "supplementary ZIP" content, delivered as a directory) |
| `results/` | The experimental results the paper's claims rest on |

`paper.tex`, `neurips_2026.sty`, `checklist.tex` and `paper.pdf` belong at the root of the
report owner's repository (the delivery root). `code/` and `results/` are delivered by the
position the work assignment gives them to, at those same paths. Everything else (figure
sources, extra files, internal notes) is free in name and layout; only the six paths above
are fixed.

The official template files to build from are in `template/` of this repository
(`neurips_2026.tex` is the venue's own formatting-instructions shell; replace author,
title, abstract and text). Verified to compile with the provided TeX Live 2026
(`pdflatex`), producing a 12-page PDF for the instructions shell.

## 2. Page limit **[binding]**

From the formatting instructions (`neurips_2026.tex`, "Style"):

> Papers may only be up to **nine** pages long, including figures. Papers that exceed the
> page limit will not be reviewed (or in any other way considered) for presentation at the
> conference.
> Additional pages *containing acknowledgments, references, checklist, and optional technical
> appendices* do not count as content pages.

Main Track Handbook, "Paper Formatting Instructions":

> The main text of a submitted paper is limited to nine content pages, including all figures
> and tables. Additional pages containing references, optional technical appendices and
> mandatory paper checklist do not count as content pages. If your submission is accepted,
> you will be allowed an additional content page for the camera-ready version. The maximum
> file size is 50MB.

So: **at most 9 content pages** (including all figures and tables); references, appendices
and the checklist are additional and unlimited. The camera-ready extra page does not apply
(this is a submission-standard delivery, not an accepted paper).

## 3. Template and style **[binding]**

- The only supported style file is `neurips_2026.sty` (LaTeX2e). "Previous style files for
  LaTeX 2.09, Microsoft Word, and RTF are no longer supported." The Word template has been
  discontinued; the LaTeX style file "is the only template we will accept".
- "Tweaking the style files may be grounds for desk rejection"; do not modify the text
  rectangle (5.5 in wide x 9 in long, 1.5 in left margin), font sizes (10 pt, 11 pt
  leading, Times New Roman), or any style parameters. "Submissions that violate the NeurIPS
  style (e.g., by decreasing margins or font sizes) or page limits may be desk rejected."
- Submission mode: `\usepackage{neurips_2026}` (default = `main` option, Main Track) with
  **neither** `final` nor `preprint` — "At submission time, please omit the `final` and
  `preprint` options. This will anonymize your submission and add line numbers to aid
  review." The `final` option is only for accepted papers; `preprint` is for arXiv-style
  non-anonymous versions. Do not refer to the line numbers in the text.

## 4. Double-blind anonymity **[binding]**

Main Track Handbook, "Double-blind Reviewing: For Authors":

> All submissions must be anonymized and may not contain any identifying information that
> may violate the double-blind reviewing policy. This policy applies to any supplementary
> or linked material as well, including code. If you are including links to any external
> material, it is your responsibility to guarantee anonymous browsing. Please do not include
> acknowledgments at submission time. If you need to cite one of your own papers, you should
  do so with adequate anonymization to preserve double-blind reviewing. For instance, write
  "In the previous work of Smith et al. [1]..." rather than "In our previous work [1]...".
  Papers violating this policy will be desk rejected.

- No author names, affiliations, acknowledgments, or identifying links anywhere, including
  `code/`.
- The `ack` environment from the style file automatically hides acknowledgments in the
  anonymized submission; do not include an acknowledgments section.
- Cite any prior work of the "authors" in third person.

## 5. NeurIPS paper checklist **[binding]**

From `checklist.tex` (official file):

> Do not remove the checklist: **The papers not including the checklist will be desk
  rejected.** The checklist should follow the references and follow the (optional)
  supplemental material. The checklist does NOT count towards the page limit.

Requirements for the delivered `checklist.tex`:

- Keep the section heading "NeurIPS Paper Checklist", the checklist subsection headings,
  questions and guidelines; **delete the instruction block** (between `%%% BEGIN
  INSTRUCTIONS %%%` and `%%% END INSTRUCTIONS %%%`) and **do not modify the questions**;
  use only the provided macros `\answerYes{}`, `\answerNo{}`, `\answerNA{}` for answers.
- Every one of the 17 questions gets an answer (Yes/No/NA) plus a short 1--2 sentence
  justification immediately after it (`\answerNA` and `\answerNo` are acceptable with a
  proper justification; `\answerTODO` in the delivered file means the checklist is not done).
- The questions cover: claims; limitations; theory assumptions and proofs; experimental
  result reproducibility; open access to data and code; experimental setting/details;
  experiment statistical significance; experiments compute resources; code of ethics;
  broader impacts; safeguards; licenses for existing assets; new assets; crowdsourcing and
  research with human subjects; IRB approvals; declaration of LLM usage.
- `\input{checklist.tex}` after `\newpage` at the end of `paper.tex`, after references and
  appendices (as the official template does).
- The paper is encouraged to include a separate "Limitations" section (checklist item 2);
  claims in the abstract and introduction must match the paper's actual contributions.

## 6. Citations and references

- `natbib` is loaded by the style file. "Citations may be author/year or numeric, as long as
  you maintain internal consistency. As to the format of the references themselves, any
  style is acceptable as long as it is used consistently."
- References: unnumbered first-level heading ("References"), after acknowledgments
  (camera-ready) / at the end of content (submission); font size may be reduced to
  `small` (9 pt); the reference section does not count toward the page limit.
- Study-specific constraint (from the objective, stricter than the venue's policy): no
  paper, preprint, code release or other written work first published after 1 September
  2026 may be used. The venue's own "contemporaneous work" rule (work appearing online
  after March 1, 2026 is contemporaneous, not prior work) does not relax the objective's
  cutoff.

## 7. PDF properties **[binding]**

- "Please prepare submission files with paper size 'US Letter,' and not, for example, 'A4.'"
- "You should directly generate PDF files using `pdflatex`."
- "Your PDF file must only contain Type 1 or Embedded TrueType fonts" (no Type 3 fonts;
  avoid `\bbold`, use `amsfonts`).
- Pages should be numbered (automatic in submission mode).
- Maximum PDF file size 50 MB.

## 8. Appendices

- Technical appendices (proofs, additional results, experimental details) go in the single
  PDF after the references, before the checklist; "do not upload a separate PDF file for the
  appendix. There is no page limit for the technical appendices."
- "Think of the appendix as 'optional reading' for reviewers. The paper must be able to
  stand alone without the appendix; for example, adding critical experiments that support
  the main claims to an appendix is inappropriate." Results needed for the main claims
  belong in the 9 content pages.
- Proofs may appear in supplemental material, but then the paper should carry a proof
  sketch; "any informal proof provided in the core of the paper should be complemented by
  formal proofs provided in appendix or supplemental material" (checklist item 3).

## 9. Code and data (`code/`, `results/`) **[binding]**

Main Track Handbook, "Code and Data Submission" / "Code Guidelines":

- Supplementary code/data is "a single ZIP file of up to 100MB", anonymized like the main
  submission. For this delivery the ZIP's content is delivered as the `code/` and `results/`
  directories instead (nothing needs to be zipped; the size guide of 100 MB still signals
  the expected scale).
- "Your code submission should include training and evaluation code, specification of
  dependencies, etc."
- "Your code submission ideally should be self-contained and executable. If this is not the
  case, you must explain why" (e.g., specialized hardware required).
- Data guidelines: if a new dataset is submitted, answer the relevant checklist questions;
  license and access restrictions should be described. The experimental scope is the plan's
  to settle: if the study uses only synthetic/self-generated experiments, the dataset items
  of the checklist are expected to be N/A; if the plan uses real data (e.g. an image dataset
  for a JEPA), then the data guidelines above apply and the dataset/license checklist items
  (licenses for existing assets, new assets) must be answered for those assets accordingly.
- For accepted papers NeurIPS asks for de-anonymized links; that does not apply here (see
  section 12).

The delivered `code/` and `results/` must be the material the paper's claims rest on:
enough to reproduce or check the paper's experiments (scripts, configurations, seeds where
fixed, and the numeric results referenced by figures and tables), with a README stating how
to run what, and which input each result was produced from.

## 10. Contribution type (submission-form field, record in the brief for the plan)

Authors choose one of: General; Theory ("The main contribution is via analyses and
proofs"); Use-Inspired; Concept & Feasibility; Negative Results. A paper whose contribution
is a theory of JEPA collapse tested empirically is most naturally **Theory** (with the
empirical tests) or **General**; the report owner should record the choice; no in-paper
markup is required (it is entered in the submission form, not the manuscript).

## 11. Dates (context, not binding on the delivery)

- Abstract deadline: May 4, 2026 (AOE); full paper deadline (including supplementary):
  May 6, 2026 (AOE); notification: September 24, 2026.
- These deadlines are in the past relative to the working date (2026-10-11); the objective
  asks for a paper that *could be submitted*, i.e. compliance with the requirements, not an
  actual submission.

## 12. What this delivery cannot establish (recorded honestly)

- No actual OpenReview submission, profile, or author list: the delivery is anonymized
  submission-standard material; OpenReview setup, conflicts of interest, institutional
  eligibility, and the submission form fields (contribution type, author entry) cannot be
  exercised here.
- No acceptance, camera-ready version, or de-anonymization step: the delivery is to
  submission standard only.
- The venue declares **no license** for anything, and NeurIPS's own dataset/model licensing
  best practices are recorded as guidance only.
- Dual submission policy: nothing about this delivery is concurrently submitted elsewhere;
  the policy binds the hypothetical submission, not this work.
- Ethics: this delivery records no position on the study's experimental scope (the plan
  settles it). If the study involves no human subjects, no crowdsourcing, no new data
  collection, and no datasets of real people, the relevant checklist items are expected to
  be NA, answered with justification; if the plan includes real datasets, answer the
  dataset and license items for those assets as above.

